<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lunéville</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lunéville is a town on the Meurthe in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meurthe-et-moselle">Meurthe-et-Moselle</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Duke Leopold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> built a large palace there from 1703, inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a>, and it remained the court of the dukes and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-leszczynski">Stanisław Leszczyński</a> until 1766. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was signed in the town in 1801.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luneville.jpg" alt="Lunéville" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Clément293 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D0A3329-Modifier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lunéville is a commune in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meurthe-et-moselle">Meurthe-et-Moselle</a> department of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a subprefecture of the department. It lies on the river Meurthe at its confluence with the Vezouze. It was a resort in the 18th century and was known as the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Château de Lunéville was built early in the 18th century for Leopold, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, to replace an older palace. It was designed in the style of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> to satisfy Leopold's wife, Élisabeth Charlotte d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, a niece of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, and became known as the Versailles of Lorraine. It includes a chapel designed by Germain Boffrand. Leopold and his wife were the parents of Prince Charles Alexander of Lorraine and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i-holy-roman-emperor">Francis I, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, through whom they were grandparents of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marie-antoinette">Marie Antoinette</a>. The last duke of Lorraine, Stanislaus I, the former king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, had a church and several follies built in his gardens for the amusement and education of visiting Polish and Lithuanian nobility and followers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><p>Visitors to his court included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>, André Morellet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>. After Stanislaus died in 1766, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a> annexed the duchy and turned the château into a barracks. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> was signed at 61 Rue de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, one of the houses built against the château gardens, on 9 February 1801 between the French Republic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a> and Count Ludwig von Cobenzl. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> of 1871 made Lunéville a border town, attracting people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a> who moved to keep their French nationality, and a period of prosperity known as the Belle Époque followed.</p><p>Lunéville faience, made from 1723 by Jacques Chambrette, became the Royal Factory of the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> after Stanislaus sponsored it in 1749. The Croismare glass factory, built in 1858, became known for art deco glass after the Müller brothers settled there in 1897.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Much of the original construction of the château has survived and is open to the public, and its parterre gardens, designed by Yves Hours in 1711 and Louis de Nesle in 1724, are a public park. Lunéville faience still exists as Terres d'Est. Among those connected with the town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet">Émilie du Châtelet</a> lived there for a long time and died there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-de-la-tour">Georges de La Tour</a> lived there for much of his career.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lunéville” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Leopold restored orderly government after 1697 from his new palace at <b>Lunéville</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The defeat forced Austria to make peace at <b>Lunéville</b> in February 1801.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-3">3 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
